    "## *R6. HMM Parameters: States, Observations, Initial Distribution, and Emission Matrix*"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "62e921fc-a29b-4f39-80a9-172145c34fb1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "In this section, we extend the project's Markov chain formulation into a *Hidden Markov Model (HMM)* so that unobservable student study states can be linked to observable academic outcomes across time. To stay consistent with the earlier sections of the notebook, we reuse the shared state definitions and transition dynamics (STATES and P) and introduce the emission structure (E).\n",
    "\n",
    "### Core HMM Definitions and Alignment with Earlier Sections\n",
    "\n",
    "The hidden states (STATES) are Focused, Distracted, and Burned Out, representing the latent study states of a student that are not directly observed. The observable outcomes (OBSERVATIONS) are High Score, Average Score, and Low / Missed, representing the recorded performance results at each step.\n",
    "\n",
    "The initial state distribution (pi_0) is [0.50, 0.30, 0.20], matching the initial distribution used earlier in the project and indicating higher initial likelihood of starting in Focused. The transition matrix (P) is aligned with the transition probabilities from the preceding sections (with a fallback definition only if the cell is run standalone), ensuring full compatibility across team components.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Emission Matrix (E) Design and Validation\n",
    "\n",
    "The emission matrix E gives the conditional probability of observing each outcome given the current hidden state, where every row is non-negative and sums to 1.00:\n",
    "\n",
    "- For Focused ([0.70, 0.20, 0.10]), probabilities lean strongly toward High Score (0.70), with smaller chances for Average Score (0.20) and Low / Missed (0.10).\n",
    "- For Distracted ([0.25, 0.45, 0.30]), probabilities center around Average Score (0.45), alongside moderate chances for Low / Missed (0.30) and High Score (0.25).\n",
    "- For Burned Out ([0.10, 0.25, 0.65]), probabilities skew heavily toward Low / Missed (0.65), with lower chances for Average Score (0.25) and High Score (0.10).\n",
    "\n",
    "We also include assert checks to verify that pi_0 sums to 1.0 and that every row of both P and E sums to 1.0.\n",
    "\n",
    "> *Note:* The probabilities in E are illustrative domain assumptions created for modeling and demonstration in this project rather than empirical measurements from real students."
   ]
